4-51e8-99ac-79d8b3081363" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Points that Lie on the Curve of the Inverse Sine Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c1536a66-ae83-5265-94f9-70be4b868ac9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109794} {:answer/id #uuid "f1302b9c-3fc8-5e61-b42b-05c0ae147955" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109795} {:answer/id #uuid "40c76d25-0c3a-5030-bcbb-a83562591705" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109796} {:answer/id #uuid "4981da33-75a4-5e5c-8a7a-5627622f1281" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109797} {:answer/id #uuid "675ab9f7-87d8-5824-8498-ff60a656458c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109798}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4981da33-75a4-5e5c-8a7a-5627622f1281" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109797} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ac2b183-e150-53fb-8f22-14592d9ad8ad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109793}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "183017f0-e71f-5632-89e1-4603485e8f26" :question/math-academy-id "q-65744" :question/problem "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y = \\arcsin x$?\n\n1. $(\\frac{π}{6}, \\frac{1}{2})$\n2. $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}, \\frac{π}{3})$\n3. $(0, 2π)$" :question/worked-solution "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x,$ the following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ meaning $a=\\operatorname{sin}⁡b.$\n\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x,$ which is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points. \n\n- First, we consider the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{1}{2}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$  $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{1}{2})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ does lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $(0,2\\pi ).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(2\\pi ,0)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ and $y=2\\pi $ does **not** lie within this range. $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(0,2\\pi )$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268481 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "76b0ad1e-827e-5795-8431-3276c8eed881" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Values in the Range of the Inverse Sine Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54968a23-9e77-5337-a3fe-a11e240a985c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268483} {:answer/id #uuid "275f90d1-53ab-52af-ab1f-045550c73774" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268484} {:answer/id #uuid "60bf0eed-4d0c-520e-8894-ba5a4d04670f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268485} {:answer/id #uuid "8e4890fe-4c4a-5cf5-868f-f0cb2a83815a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268486} {:answer/id #uuid "20244539-539e-566e-bb43-65d96c92faed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268487}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "20244539-539e-566e-bb43-65d96c92faed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268487} :answer-field/id #uuid "45143a1b-f871-5f2c-9f35-a345412a613b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268482}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5f0c58c7-df1e-5923-b8a7-531c73c4f592" :question/math-academy-id "q-65809" :question/problem "Which of the following values is in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13962359/assets/df22eea4331c47da3047381ca672fd80ebf75118054316fe88c0e2d96dae0d34.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The range of the function is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$\n\nTherefore, the value $y=\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ is in the range of the function."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268488 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "76b0ad1e-827e-5795-8431-3276c8eed881" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Values in the Range of the Inverse Sine Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46bafc67-9890-52ce-9e5e-d311c472ef2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268490} {:answer/id #uuid "d23120a5-7c70-565d-989d-90d8f4b8011f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-\\frac{\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268491} {:answer/id #uuid "0506f736-e4a4-5749-8f9d-3babe9e70bbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,-\\frac{\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268492} {:answer/id #uuid "8c3ebc08-b9a7-56dd-bb02-b3c4b533d519" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268493} {:answer/id #uuid "f050a153-b211-59f7-bc2f-40e849dde683" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,-\\frac{\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268494}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f050a153-b211-59f7-bc2f-40e849dde683" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,-\\frac{\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268494} :answer-field/id #uuid "5dcbde64-4d56-5c73-8583-2330f7e1e357" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268489}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "109fbc08-f95d-5f24-a47b-5ea5c8baa367" :question/math-academy-id "q-65811" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the coordinates of the minimum point of the function $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13962359/assets/df22eea4331c47da3047381ca672fd80ebf75118054316fe88c0e2d96dae0d34.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the minimum of the function is $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and it is reached at $x=-1.$ Therefore, the coordinates of the minimum point of the function are $(-1,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268495 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "67692569-e3a4-51e8-99ac-79d8b3081363" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Points that Lie on the Curve of the Inverse Sine Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c72fd68b-fd68-56e7-8af9-bb5c0c0a29b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268497} {:answer/id #uuid "de98d5a0-a32f-5926-ae00-726b19affc45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268498} {:answer/id #uuid "48f72946-ae8f-52c5-9ae3-9f6f8822e2ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268499} {:answer/id #uuid "bd396485-e5c9-5fee-9074-3793ec0d79e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268500} {:answer/id #uuid "fed88f98-cb19-5e13-9adc-ef337c274293" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268501}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c72fd68b-fd68-56e7-8af9-bb5c0c0a29b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268497} :answer-field/id #uuid "7af8c36f-78c8-5256-88f8-d88ef74d5e96" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268496}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "23074372-05ee-554d-b915-2199d9c252b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-65821" :question/problem "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x?$\n\n- $(0,-2\\pi )$\n- $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{6})$\n- $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{4})$" :question/worked-solution "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x,$ the following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ meaning $a=\\operatorname{sin}⁡b.$\n\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x,$ which is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points. \n\n- First, we consider the point $(0,-2\\pi ).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(-2\\pi ,0)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ and $y=-2\\pi $ does **not** lie within this range. $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(0,-2\\pi )$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{1}{2})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(-\\frac{\\pi }{4},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ and $y=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268502 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "67692569-e3a4-51e8-99ac-79d8b3081363" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Points that Lie on the Curve of the Inverse Sine Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bc62b01d-3d22-5c1d-80ac-5fc240f66201" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268504} {:answer/id #uuid "9839868e-cde7-58d5-bced-55c6e63113f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268505} {:answer/id #uuid "e7d25c4b-dd16-552d-8824-76bef32ad7f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268506} {:answer/id #uuid "0326e494-3ee5-574e-89a3-c74e7a1a75d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268507} {:answer/id #uuid "9d009e31-b413-572e-a528-fb0c4846a404" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268508}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9839868e-cde7-58d5-bced-55c6e63113f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268505} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd0f6401-d152-533e-928b-3d5a0212f9ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268503}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c75e97a9-af1e-5da5-a058-c8850b93da12" :question/math-academy-id "q-65822" :question/problem "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x?$\n\n- $(0,-\\pi )$\n- $(-1,\\frac{3\\pi }{2})$\n- $(-\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{6})$" :question/worked-solution "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x,$ the following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ meaning $a=\\operatorname{sin}⁡b.$\n\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x,$ which is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points.  \n\n- First, we consider the point $(0,-\\pi ).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(-\\pi ,0)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ and $y=-\\pi $ does **not** lie within this range. $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(0,-\\pi )$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $(-1,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{3\\pi }{2},-1)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ and $y=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ does **not** lie within this range. $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(-1,\\frac{3\\pi }{2})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $(-\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(-\\frac{\\pi }{6},-\\frac{1}{2})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ and $y=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268509 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "69d6ed5b-1c29-53a7-a37e-817042a02861" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing the Inverse Sine Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f751c7b3-7f92-532c-9a22-a4f9f82f53ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13962359/assets/aa8e3fbeb248c814d2b8b58a9472d8b9eb280ece7a36c1eaa312a8db39d0e92d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268511} {:answer/id #uuid "89b1f24c-9db0-54fa-84fe-2ec4b06d47d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13962359/assets/0a218c9121e2561ad5c5fca0f6f8a752147d42d1b4eee9bcf6e9c4e414fe96f5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268512} {:answer/id #uuid "45703042-16cb-5e36-9535-8f1ea52cb36e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathaca