r/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268527} {:answer/id #uuid "48a594f3-8bbd-593b-92a9-7ce1bd7c74c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268528} {:answer/id #uuid "4992a1b1-a44c-53f0-b1c2-58d84af1f3cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi -3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268529}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d1ab3020-87b3-5b63-a9f6-3fcabcaf9121" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268525} :answer-field/id #uuid "56c0d3d5-f743-56c7-81d9-b3ab67669797" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268524}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1d0e8c0e-168e-581f-b23a-c7a0c10fdced" :question/math-academy-id "q-70619" :question/problem "Which of the following values is **not** in the domain of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13962359/assets/df22eea4331c47da3047381ca672fd80ebf75118054316fe88c0e2d96dae0d34.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The domain of the function is $x\\in [-1,1].$\n\nTherefore, the value $x=\\pi -1$ is not in the domain of the function."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268530 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "67692569-e3a4-51e8-99ac-79d8b3081363" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Points that Lie on the Curve of the Inverse Sine Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f9cda64-ead8-5ef2-bd29-60c064c03432" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268532} {:answer/id #uuid "ba84f6d4-009d-523d-93d9-f03bffd1bd06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268533} {:answer/id #uuid "8ae4f08e-3df6-512b-8bf9-d1e86b010994" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268534} {:answer/id #uuid "529eede8-a76e-51b0-8e24-ce66b0949106" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268535} {:answer/id #uuid "16edb07f-f0eb-594d-a358-a7dd750a3b24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268536}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16edb07f-f0eb-594d-a358-a7dd750a3b24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268536} :answer-field/id #uuid "1833c17a-3106-5f66-8bfe-5acada24124e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268531}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a72ce64f-755c-5a29-9c7b-cd49fb251531" :question/math-academy-id "q-70714" :question/problem "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x?$\n\n- $(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{2})$\n- $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$\n- $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{6})$" :question/worked-solution "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x,$ the following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ meaning $a=\\operatorname{sin}⁡b.$\n\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x,$ which is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points.  \n\n- First, we consider the point $(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$  $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{2})$  does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},1)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$  $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{6})$  does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$"}]}
